- op: create  # 60cc
  at: '2026-08-04T06:22:44Z'  # 60cc
  by: agent  # 60cc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 60cc
  clock: 1  # 60cc
  nonce: 60cc  # 60cc
  data:  # 60cc
    kind: invariant  # 60cc
    title: "Section 3a cannot refute because 40% of escape sites are not calls (field/container/closure), which section 7b excludes"  # 60cc
    status: violated  # 60cc
    priority: 1  # 60cc
    parent:  # 60cc
    tags:  # 60cc
    - taint  # 60cc
    - dataflow  # 60cc
    - adr_0017  # 60cc
    isbefore: []  # 60cc
    duplicate_of: []  # 60cc
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 60cc
    pr_ref:  # 60cc
    description: "MEASURED BLOCKER, not a hypothesis. ADR-0017 section 4 function summaries are\nnow wired and the python/go catalogues cover every enumerated callee a\nqualified-name catalogue can express (PR #197), and section 3a still refutes\nNOTHING. The reason is this item.\n\nWHAT WAS MEASURED (cohort: caddy, alertmanager, pretix, kserve, zoxide):\n  264 escape sites where the section 3a walk lost track of a tainted value\n   107 (40.5%)  NO CALL AT THE LINE AT ALL\n    64 (24.2%)  callee module unresolved (external.*)\n    26 ( 9.8%)  first-party in-repo callee\n    67 (25.4%)  stdlib/third-party -- a declared catalogue can name these\n    25 ( 9.5%)  ...and of those, actually terminate the taint\n\n  A/B on the same binary, catalogue live vs forced empty:\n    16 confirmed / 40 unknown / 0 refuted -- IDENTICAL in both arms.\n\nWHY 25 CLOSED SITES BUY ZERO FLOWS. A walk returns unknown if ANY branch\nescapes, so a flow becomes refutable only when EVERY escape on it closes. Of\n37 unknown walks, 5 did close at least one site and every one of them still\nhad another open. Per-walk anatomy of what held them open:\n    81  no call at line\n    40  catalogued but propagates\n    21  callee module unresolved\n    12  uncatalogued callee\nAnd at the margin -- the 14 walks held open by exactly ONE site, i.e. the ones\nnearest to refutable -- the single blocker is \"no call at the line\" in 10 of\n14. Fixing everything else on this list still would not refute those 10.\n\nWHAT \"NO CALL AT THE LINE\" MEANS. The tainted value is used at a line that\ndefines nothing the DDG tracked AND invokes nothing: a field write\n(self.x = tainted), a container subscript (d[k] = tainted), a closure capture.\nThere is no callee to look up, so no function summary can ever help. This is\nADR-0017 section 7a field-sensitivity / alias analysis territory, and section\n7b EXPLICITLY EXCLUDES alias analysis and whole-program analysis.\n\nSO THIS IS A SCOPE COLLISION, NOT A BUG. Section 3a's removal authority\nrequires distinguishing \"the taint stopped here\" from \"I lost it here\", and\nfor 40% of escape sites that distinction needs precisely the capability\nsection 7b declines to build. Three honest options, none of them free:\n  (a) Narrow refutation's DOMAIN: refute only flows whose every escape site is\n      a call with a catalogued terminating summary. Sound, and by this\n      measurement it addresses 0 of 51 walks today -- so it would ship as an\n      inert capability unless the other blockers move first.\n  (b) Implement enough field-sensitivity to track a value into a field or\n      container slot within one function (section 7a is \"field-sensitivity\n      LITE\" and already partially exists). Reopens the section 7b line.\n  (c) Accept confirm-only permanently and RETIRE the removal goal, updating\n      WI-kabif and INV-sadah to match what the analysis can actually do.\nDo not pick one from the armchair -- (b)'s size is unmeasured, and that\nmeasurement is the next step for this item.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR ADJACENT ITEMS, stated so they are not closed on this PR:\n * INV-sadah stays violated. Its statement requires INCLUSION be decided by\n   data-dependence information; the walk still only CONFIRMS.\n * WI-kabif stays todo_hard. Its filed list of four prerequisites was already\n   six (section 4 summaries, per-call-site lines); this is the SEVENTH, and\n   unlike the others it is not a wiring gap but a declared scope exclusion."  # 60cc
    fields:  # 60cc
      statement: "For the ADR-0017 section 3a walk to license removing a flow, every point at which it loses track of a tainted value must be attributable to a callee whose summary says the taint terminates. Today 40.5% of those points are not calls at all."  # 60cc
      root_cause: "A tainted value used at a line that defines nothing AND invokes nothing (field write, container subscript, closure capture) leaves no callee to consult, so no function summary can resolve it. Distinguishing \"stopped here\" from \"lost here\" at those sites requires field-sensitivity/alias analysis, which ADR-0017 section 7b explicitly excludes."  # 60cc
- op: discuss  # 9814
  at: '2026-08-04T07:19:30Z'  # 9814
  by: agent  # 9814
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9814
  clock: 2  # 9814
  nonce: '9814'  # 9814
  message: "REFRAMED after comparing against Joern (owner-endorsed 2026-08-04). The three\noptions filed above are superseded by a fourth; keeping them recorded because\nthe reasoning that ruled them out is the useful part.\n\nWHAT JOERN DOES, verified from docs rather than memory:\n * Its taint query is REACHABILITY over the dependence layer of the CPG. There\n   is no \"I lost the trail\" state at all -- either a path exists or it does not.\n * For a method with no declared semantics, verbatim: \"Joern will treat\n   external methods with no semantic definitions as able to propagate taint\n   from all arguments, to all arguments including the return value.\" That is\n   total OVER-TAINT -- exactly what hypergumbo's get_default_summary does and\n   exactly what I deliberately refused to use in PR #197.\n * Its semantics language is a NARROWING device: `\"foo\" 1->-1 2->3` where -1 is\n   the return and 0 the receiver, and \"unmapped argument pairs are treated as\n   killed\". Writing an entry kills the flows you did not list.\n NOT VERIFIED: whether reachableByFlows follows control dependence (implicit\n flow) or data dependence only. The docs page did not say and I am not\n asserting it from memory; it bears on how condition-reads should be treated.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY THAT EXPLAINS OUR ZERO. Same ingredients, opposite economics:\n\n                  | Joern                    | hypergumbo today\n  unknown callee  | assume taint flows       | refuse to judge\n  catalogue does  | narrows, entry by entry  | enables judgment, all-or-nothing\n  payoff curve    | INCREMENTAL              | ZERO until 100% of a walk\n\nOur catalogue is not incomplete -- PR #197 covers every enumerated name a\nqualified-name catalogue can express. The model is what makes it worthless: a\nwalk needs EVERY escape closed simultaneously before one flow moves. Joern's\nmodel pays out on the first entry written.\n\nTHE FOURTH OPTION: MAKE DEPENDENCE REACHABILITY DECIDE INCLUSION.\nRather than call-graph BFS deciding inclusion and section 3a bolting a label\non top, compose per-function summaries along the call graph and let the\ncomposed dependence path be the inclusion criterion. Concretely:\n  1. section 4a infers a summary for each in-repo function from its own DDG.\n  2. At a call site, taint crosses into the callee only where the callee's\n     summary says the argument flows onward.\n  3. AT EVERY POINT OF UNCERTAINTY, OVER-TAINT. If the walk inside F escapes,\n     F's inferred summary says \"the parameter reaches the return\". If F has no\n     DDG at all, same. If the callee is external and uncatalogued, same.\nThis is the safety property that makes it different from the arm that deleted\nthree real flows: removal is then licensed by a summary that SAYS no flow, and\nthe summary over-approximates whenever we lost track. A lost trail produces\n\"flows\", never \"does not flow\" -- so a lost trail can no longer cause a removal.\n\nIt also degrades gracefully. A language with no DDG over-taints everywhere and\nlands exactly on today's structural behaviour, which is what already happens.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT FREE. Joern's dataflow is INTERPROCEDURAL; ours is\nintraprocedural and line-keyed. Making dependence-reachability decide inclusion\nwith today's intraprocedural DDG alone would kill every cross-function flow --\nabout 69% of them by WI-kabif's own measurement -- which is a false-negative\ncatastrophe. The composition step (2) is precisely what buys that back, and it\nis the part that does not exist yet. section 4a's `infer_summary` has zero\nproduction callers.\n\nNEXT STEP, and it is a MEASUREMENT not a build: implement section 4a inference\nwith over-taint-on-uncertainty and measure, over the cohort, what fraction of\nin-repo functions on taint paths yield a CONFIDENT \"this parameter does not\nreach the return\". If that fraction is near zero the whole direction is dead\nand we learn it cheaply. If it is substantial, the composition is worth\nbuilding. Do not build composition before that number exists."  # 9814
- op: discuss  # dd84
  at: '2026-08-05T17:51:31Z'  # dd84
  by: agent  # dd84
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dd84
  clock: 3  # dd84
  nonce: dd84  # dd84
  message: "RE-MEASURED 2026-08-05 on dev 5993042f18. THE FINDING REPRODUCES; ONE OF ITS\nFOUR CATEGORIES IS NO LONGER MEASURABLE THE WAY IT WAS MEASURED.\n\nMETHOD. Same cohort (caddy, alertmanager, pretix, kserve, zoxide), same claims\nfile, and the SAME instrument -- walk_anatomy.py, wrapping _use_site_terminates\nand _ddg_taint_reaches. The classification block was copied byte-identical so\nthe two runs stay comparable; only plumbing changed, and it had to, because the\noriginal crashes against today's signature: #203 added defs_at / inherits and\n#214 added barrier_lines plus a SECOND walk per candidate flow. Barrier walks\nare tagged and excluded from the population -- counting them would inflate the\ndenominator against the filed number. All five repos re-analyzed from scratch\n(\"No cached results found\" in every run), so this is not a stale read.\n\n*** THE CORRECTION, AND IT INVERTS THE OBVIOUS READING. *** A naive re-run\nreports \"no call at line\" rising 40.5% -> 65.7% and \"callee module unresolved\"\nfalling 21 -> ZERO, which reads as one category solved and the blocker grown.\nBOTH HALVES ARE ARTIFACTS OF INV-rozaj (#200), WHICH LANDED AFTER THIS\nMEASUREMENT. #200 routes _qualified_callee's module half through\n_module_from_symbol_path, which returns empty for the \"external\" placeholder,\nso such a callee now yields NO qualified name at all and never reaches\ncallee_names. The instrument's external-prefix test therefore CANNOT MATCH BY\nCONSTRUCTION, and its population moved silently into the neighbouring bucket.\nVerified on caddy: its 48 \"no call at line\" split 37 genuine / 11 call-present-\nbut-unresolved, totals reconciling at 90 either way.\n\nCORRECTED PER-WALK ANATOMY, unknown walks only, against the filed 81/40/21/12:\n\n    category                          today   share      filed   share\n    no call at line (genuine)            86   51.8%         81   52.6%\n    catalogued but propagates            40   24.1%         40   26.0%\n    call present, callee unresolved      23   13.9%         21   13.6%\n    uncatalogued callee                  17   10.2%         12    7.8%\n                                        166 over 40 walks  154 over 37 walks\n\nMARGIN IDENTICAL. Walks held open by exactly ONE site: 14, against the filed\n14; the single blocker is a genuine non-call site in 10 of 14, against the\nfiled 10 of 14. That is the number this item's conclusion rested on and it\nreproduced to the unit.\n\nVERDICTS UNCHANGED: 16 confirmed / 40 unknown / 0 REFUTED over 56 walks.\nSection 3a still refutes nothing. The identity is structural rather than\ncoincidental -- #209's catalogue growth was entirely cpp/kotlin/scala/elixir,\nnone of which this cohort exercises, and #207's rust fix lands on zoxide, which\nproduces zero walks.\n\nWHAT GENUINELY MOVED. Total escape sites 264 -> 225 (200 open + 25 closed).\nThe 25 closed by a terminating summary is unchanged, as is the stdlib/third-\nparty total of 67. \"callee module unresolved\" roughly HALVED, 64 -> 33 on the\nall-sites denominator; I have NOT established the cause and am not guessing --\nthe obvious candidate (#207's path-slot chokepoint) targets rust, and rust\ncontributes zero walks here, so that explanation does not hold.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S STATEMENT FIGURE IS SUPERSEDED. It reads \"Today 40.5% of those\npoints are not calls at all.\" On the same denominator (all sites consulted,\n225) the genuine non-call share is 47.1%. The statement was not edited; this\nnote is the correction of record.\n\n*** TWO NUMBERS THE ORIGINAL DID NOT COMPUTE, AND THEY ARE THE DECISION. ***\n    20 of 40 unknown walks have a genuine non-call site as their ONLY open\n       category -- unreachable by ANY amount of catalogue or summary work.\n     4 of 40 have no genuine non-call site at all -- that is the entire\n       population better summaries could ever refute.\nSo PERFECT section 4 coverage buys at most 4 of 40 walks. That is a far sharper\nanswer to \"is catalogue growth worth it\" than a 40.5% share, and it argues the\nsame direction as this item's own fourth option: the payoff is not in the\ncatalogue, it is in changing the model.\n\nFOR WHOEVER RE-RUNS THIS NEXT. The external-prefix bucket is dead as a\npredicate. Split \"no call at line\" by consulting a RAW call index built from\nproduction's _edge_call_sites -- every (caller, line) carrying a call edge at\nall, qualified or not -- or the zero will read as progress again. Instruments\nkept at scratchpad/walk_anatomy2.py (plumbing-updated original) and\nwalk_anatomy3.py (the split)."  # dd84
- op: discuss  # de31
  at: '2026-08-05T19:17:59Z'  # de31
  by: agent  # de31
  actor: jgstern_agent  # de31
  clock: 4  # de31
  nonce: de31  # de31
  message: "THE FOURTH OPTION WAS PUT THROUGH ADVERSARIAL DESIGN REVIEW 2026-08-05\n(three fresh-context lenses, one charged with refutation, per the standing\npanel rule) BEFORE implementation. Outcome: DO NOT BUILD AS SPECIFIED, and\nthe reason is a predicate defect at its step 2, not an implementation cost.\n\nTHE DEFECT, checkable without a cohort run: step 2 gates a call-graph hop on\nthe callee's param_to_return -- \"taint crosses into the callee only where the\nsummary says the argument flows onward\". But a parameter that dies at a SINK\ninside the callee yields a genuine, sound \"does not reach the return\":\n\n    def h(x):  write(x)          # x never reaches h's return\n    v = source(); h(v)           # the flow to report\n\nOver-taint-on-uncertainty gives ZERO protection here because nothing was\nlost -- the walk legitimately exhausts. \"Does not reach the RETURN\" is simply\nnot the predicate \"does not flow onward\": it errs in the removal-licensing\ndirection on exactly the flows a taint tool exists to report. Composition\ntherefore needs summaries that carry SINK-REACHABILITY (does any argument\nreach a catalogued sink or an onward call inside the callee), a different\nand strictly larger artifact than section 4a's return-reachability.\n\nTHE 0.00% MEASUREMENT THAT CAME OUT OF THE SAME EXERCISE IS INVALID -- DO\nNOT CITE IT. A design agent ran the confident-negative measurement on two\ncorpora and reported 0.00% in both. Its instrument passed NO callee index:\nwith callees_at empty, _use_site_terminates returns False on its first\nline, no escape can ever close, and a confident False is structurally\nunreachable -- the number is a tautology of the instrument's configuration\n(the same shape as the earlier \"section 4 changes zero flows\" tautology).\nThe measurement lens verified that wiring the shipped catalogue into the\nidentical instrument moves the figure off zero on both corpora, and that\nthe two corpora's denominators differ 3x in composition (near half of one\nis \"no return line recoverable\"), so the two rates were not rates of the\nsame thing. Any future run of this measurement needs: callees_at and\nsummaries actually passed; a positive control demonstrating the instrument\nCAN emit a confident negative on a known shape; and a denominator\nrestricted to parameter-questions that could produce the numerator.\n\nALSO SURFACED BY THE SAME REVIEW AND FILED SEPARATELY (see the walk-holes\nwork item): two verified routes to an unearned False in the SHIPPED walk --\na seen-set cycle that drains the frontier unaccounted, and the\nfollowed=True heir step closing a dual-role statement with no escape check.\nBoth reproduce on dev with empty catalogues. Any inference built on this\nwalk inherits both until they are fixed, which is a further reason the\nmeasure-then-build order was right.\n\nSTATUS UNCHANGED: still violated, still the seventh prerequisite blocking\nWI-kabif. What changed is that the fourth option's next step is no longer\n\"implement 4a inference and measure\" -- it is \"respecify step 2's predicate\n(sink-reachability, not return-reachability), fix the walk's two False\nroutes, then design the measurement with the three controls above\"."  # de31
- op: discuss  # 675f
  at: '2026-08-05T21:38:06Z'  # 675f
  by: agent  # 675f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 675f
  clock: 5  # 675f
  nonce: 675f  # 675f
  message: "B1b SHAPE SPLIT MEASURED for the first time (2026-08-05, dev c675fc484f). The result does NOT match this item statement three named shapes, and it re-prices the section 7a/7b ruling substantially downward.\n\nINSTRUMENT: walk_anatomy4, an extension of walk_anatomy3. It wraps production build_function_cfg and retains the REAL FunctionCfg objects, so every node_type below is production own CFG parse read verbatim from CfgStatement, and defines/uses are the real def/use extractor output. What is MINE and arguable is only the node_type-to-shape naming; the histogram stands independent of it.\n\nPOSITIVE CONTROL PASSED before any new number was believed: caddy 16 walks 10 unknown / 6 confirmed and 35 genuine non-call blockers; pretix 46 genuine. Both reproduce the filed counts exactly. An earlier draft read 37 for caddy because it accumulated sites over ALL walks; walk_anatomy3 sums over UNKNOWN non-barrier walks only. Fixed and re-run; the 35 above is post-fix.\n\nCOVERAGE: 81 of the filed 86 genuine non-call blockers (94.2%). caddy 35 + pretix 46 + zoxide 0. alertmanager and kserve are NOT on this box, so 5 blockers are unmeasured.\n\nTHE SPLIT (all 81 sites / production-path subset 42):\n  expression read - assert          32 (39.5%) / 0\n  expression read - binary          20 (24.7%) / 15\n  expression read - defer            8 ( 9.9%) / 8\n  expression read - return           7 ( 8.6%) / 6\n  FIELD WRITE                        7 ( 8.6%) / 7 (16.7%)\n  expression read - boolean          4 ( 4.9%) / 4\n  bare read                          3 ( 3.7%) / 2\n  CONTAINER SUBSCRIPT                0 / 0\n  CAPTURE-MEDIATED CLOSURE           0 / 0\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE RULING: this item names field write, container subscript and closure capture. Those account for 8.6% of all measured sites and 16.7% of production-path sites. The ONLY shape behind the ADR-0017 section 7b exclusion line - capture-mediated closure - is ZERO in the measured population. So the section 7a/7b ruling, which INV-sadah S2 and INV-karud a2 were both said to run through, addresses at most one sixth of the blockers and nothing at all that section 7b forbids.\n\nWHAT THE POPULATION ACTUALLY IS: 78.6% of production-path sites are EXPRESSION READS - the tainted value is read inside an assert, a binary expression, a return or a defer, and is not stored into anything. That is not a field-sensitivity gap. It suggests the escape accounting does not distinguish \"the value flowed somewhere I cannot follow\" from \"the value was read in an expression that stores nothing\". A read in x == y is not an escape. If that reading holds, the fix is cheaper than section 7a field sensitivity AND is not gated by section 7b at all.\n\nTHE FIELD WRITES, for the record, are production code and are the declared-in-scope section 7a case verbatim: invoice.foreign_currency_rate_date = rate.source_date, with production def/use recording defines=[invoice] - the base object, field granularity absent. That is exactly what section 7a specifies and is_field_tainted (zero production callers) would provide.\n\nCAVEATS, all load-bearing:\n  1. 81 of 86; 5 unmeasured because two cohort repos are absent from this box.\n  2. Two repos, two languages (go, python), of the filed five-repo cohort.\n  3. Ran against a WARM behavior-map cache; the filed 2026-08-05 run re-analyzed from scratch. Walk counts reproduce exactly, but the cache state differs.\n  4. The test-vs-production split uses MY path regex, NOT production is_test_file. Provisional and should be re-run through the production classifier - note that ALL 32 assert sites fall in test paths, so this split is doing real work and deserves the better predicate.\n  5. The 7 field writes are roughly 3 distinct source expressions repeated across walks, not 7 independent code sites. Treat 7 as a site-instance count, not a defect count.\n  6. access_mode on these sites is structurally absent, not measured-absent: a genuine non-call site is DEFINED as having no edge at that (src,line) in lang_edges, and access_mode lives on edges. There is nothing there to carry one. Recording this so the zero is not later cited as evidence about access_mode coverage - it is a tautology of the bucket definition, the same shape as this item own invalidated 0.00% confident-negative figure.\n\nARTIFACTS: walk_anatomy4 and the per-repo JSON currently live in a session scratchpad. Per this item own durability hazard they should land in-repo before being relied on again."  # 675f
- op: discuss  # c610
  at: '2026-08-06T11:30:37Z'  # c610
  by: agent  # c610
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c610
  clock: 6  # c610
  nonce: c610  # c610
  message: "S2 MEASUREMENT, 2026-08-06. A LARGE SHARE OF THE \"GENUINE NON-CALL ESCAPE SITES\" ARE CALLS - specifically calls INV-mumov made invisible. This is a causal link between two items whose threads do not mention each other.\n\nMETHOD, and it fixes three things the filed run flagged as provisional. (1) Escape sites now come FROM THE WALK: _ddg_taint_reaches takes an escape_sites out-param, so nothing re-derives the escape logic outside the walk - the filed instrument (walk_anatomy4) is in a dead scratchpad and no longer matches the signature, which this item records as its own durability hazard. (2) node_type is read verbatim off production CfgStatement, matched to the function by its RECORDED SPAN so a neighbouring function at the same line cannot supply it. (3) test/production split via production paths.is_test_file, not a regex.\n\nRESULT on hypergumbo self-analysis, 150 escape sites, 66 resolved to a CFG statement (64 production-path):\n    call (node_type is literally `call`)   24  36.4%\n    expr read - binary                     13  19.7%\n    assignment / field write               13  19.7%\n    expr read - boolean                     6   9.1%\n    not_operator                            6   9.1%\n    bare read                               3   4.5%\n    return                                  1   1.5%\nAnd separately: 99 of 150 escape sites (66.0%) have NO callee recorded at that line, with a positive control proving the argument is live (subprocess.run IS recorded at other sites).\n\nTHE MECHANISM, read off the sites themselves: print(, isinstance(stderr, bytes), cmd.append(\"-q\") are unambiguously CALLS with no callees_at entry. That is INV-mumov verbatim - python emits ZERO call edges for attribute-chain receivers and none for a builtin bare Name. No call edge means no callees_at entry, which means _use_site_terminates cannot decide, which means escaped=True. So INV-mumov is MANUFACTURING blockers for this item. Fixing it (already filed, P1, a false-NEGATIVE fix) removes them from this population without touching 7a or 7b.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE 7a/7b RULING: it is re-priced DOWN again. On this sample the expression-read family (binary+boolean+not+bare) is 42.4%, not 78.6%, and the largest single bucket is not an escape category at all.\n\nCAVEATS, all load-bearing. Python ONLY - the three Go repos on this box yielded 15 resolved sites between them, far too few to report as shares, and caddy/pretix (where the filed 81 came from) are NOT on this box. Self-analysis, not a neutral corpus. My assignment bucket does NOT separate plain assignment from field write, so the 19.7% is an upper bound on field writes. The node_type-to-shape naming is mine; raw node types are published alongside so the histogram stands without it.\n\nINSTRUMENT ERROR RECORDED because it nearly produced a headline: a first run read positional arg a[5] for callees_at and got a clean 100%-have-no-callee. a[5] is summaries; callees_at is a[4]. The flat 100% is what gave it away.\n\nInstruments: meas/escape_shapes.py + escape_{alertmanager,prometheus,kserve,hypergumbo}.log."  # c610
- op: discuss  # 10d9
  at: '2026-08-06T17:19:54Z'  # 10d9
  by: agent  # 10d9
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 10d9
  clock: 7  # 10d9
  nonce: 10d9  # 10d9
  message: "S4 RE-SCOPING, 2026-08-06. THE POPULATION THIS ITEM DESCRIBES IS ~4x SMALLER\nTHAN MEASURED, ITS LARGEST FAMILY IS NOT THE ONE FILED, AND THE ARM THAT WOULD\nCONSUME A FIX NEVER RUNS. Instruments now IN-REPO per this item's own\ndurability hazard: scripts/measure-escape-shapes.py and\nscripts/measure-call-escape-cause.py.\n\nMETHOD. The walk names its own sites (escape_sites, PR #230) and each site now\ncarries WHICH of the four escaped=True branches fired (EscapeSite.reason,\nclosed vocabulary ESCAPE_REASONS). Node types and quoted text are production's\nown CfgStatement.node_type / code_snippet. Cohort: hypergumbo (python, self),\npretix (python, NEUTRAL), prometheus + alertmanager (go), apollo-server (ts),\nzoxide (rust). Rubric pre-registered before any number was read.\n\n*** CORRECTION TO THE STANDING CORPUS CAVEAT. *** Every share in this chain has\ncarried \"caddy and pretix are not on this box\". FALSE for pretix. That was read\noff ~/repos (6 repos); the corpus is ~/ALL_REPOS (271 in one subdirectory).\npretix -- source of 46 of the filed 81 sites, and NEUTRAL rather than\nself-analysis -- has been available throughout, as have apollo-server and\nzoxide. caddy and express are genuinely absent. The python-only self-analysis\ncaveat was avoidable, not forced.\n\n*** THE MEASUREMENT ERROR THIS ITEM ALREADY DOCUMENTED, REPEATED. *** The walk\nreturns True the moment a tainted value is used at a sink line, but escapes\nrecorded BEFORE that stay in the out-param. A walk that CONFIRMED a flow still\nreports escape sites, and those blocked NOTHING. This item's own 2026-08-05\nnote says so (\"walk_anatomy3 sums over UNKNOWN non-barrier walks only\"); the\nnew instrument's first version did not restrict, and a control added for an\nunrelated reason is what caught it. pretix, production: 52 sites over all walks\nvs 15 over BLOCKED walks -- and the largest family INVERTS, calls to field\nwrites.\n\nBLOCKER SITES (walks that returned None), 147 resolved / 81 production-path:\n    expression reads (condition reads)          32\n    calls (emission or receiver typing)         28\n    field writes / def-use gaps                 19\n    return, seeding gap                          2\nhypergumbo self-analysis supplies 63 of the 81. On the THREE NEUTRAL repos the\nproduction count is 18, in near-equal thirds -- a count, not a base for shares.\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT DECIDES THIS ITEM: THE BARRIER ARM NEVER RUNS. ***\n    repo          walk invocations   3a arm   BARRIER arm\n    hypergumbo          36             36          0\n    pretix              35             35          0\n    prometheus           7              7          0\n    alertmanager         3              3          0\nThe 3a arm tests \"is True\", so False and None COLLAPSE there. Only the barrier\narm reads False, where it earns sanitized and drops the flow. So EVERY proposal\nbuilt on reclassifying escape sites -- option (b) field-sensitivity, the\nexpression-read reclassification, anything that converts an escape into an\naccounted-for step -- is STRUCTURALLY INERT on this corpus. It cannot delete a\nfinding and cannot add one. The barrier arm runs only when 3a already returned\nTrue AND the source function calls a catalogued sanitizer, and the shipped\nsanitizer vocabulary is crypto-only. That reproduces INV-karud clause (b)'s\n\"0 registered sanitizer call sites across 95,950 taint call edges\" from the\nopposite direction.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE SUPPRESSION HAZARD that has governed this whole area: \"a\nFalse earns sanitized, so removing blockers deletes findings\" is REAL but NOT\nYET LIVE. A sanitizer catalogue is what arms it. Catalogue work CREATES the\ndanger rather than relieving it, which reverses the usual ordering intuition.\n\nREACH OF THE RECLASSIFICATION PROPOSAL, measured per WALK rather than per site,\nbecause a walk stays None if ANY of its sites escapes: 32 of 81 production\nblocker sites, but 3 of 49 blocked walks (6.1%); 1 of 21 on production-only\nsites. A per-site share systematically overstates what a per-site fix buys --\nwhich is this item's own structural point, applied to the newest candidate.\n\nTHE CALL BUCKET: NEITHER PRIOR ACCOUNT WAS RIGHT, AND THE TWO REPOS DISAGREE.\nmeasure-call-escape-cause.py runs each call-node escape through the four stages\nit can drop out at. Positive control on pretix: 443,296 edges offered to\n_is_taint_call_edge, 191,264 accepted, 69 summary entries, 31,913 callees_at\nentries -- no stage empty, so no cause is reachable by construction.\n\n    cause                                        pretix(17)   hypergumbo(24)\n    1  no edge emitted (INV-mumov)                    0         13 (54.2%)\n    3a no receiver type, external placeholder        13 (76.5%)  4 (16.7%)\n    3b resolved first-party, provenance-gated         1          0   <- CORRECT\n    4a key present, no summary (catalogue gap)        2          7 (29.2%)\n    4b summary says propagates (a REAL escape)        1          0\n\nThe two repos separate the two halves of INV-mumov. pretix's sites are all\nobj.method() attribute chains -- the half PR #231 fixed, so edges exist and the\nblock moved downstream to receiver TYPING. hypergumbo's are print(...),\nisinstance(...), hasattr(...) -- builtin bare names, the half deliberately left\nunfixed. So #231 worked for its target shape; emission is NOT \"done\".\n\nMY PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION FOR THIS BUCKET WAS REFUTED and is recorded as\nsuch: the rubric called every call-node escape \"a missing call EDGE\". On pretix\nthat is zero of seventeen.\n\nAND FIXING RECEIVER TYPING WOULD MOSTLY NOT CLOSE THESE SITES. Of the three\npretix sites that DID get a key, two hit \"no summary\" and one hit \"propagates\".\nvoucher_list.append(tainted) is a GENUINE escape -- the value enters a\ncontainer -- and is one of the three real leaks the removal-authority trial\nfound. Typing makes these escapes explicable, not absent.\n\n7a / 7b, PRICED A THIRD TIME. The assignment family is 19 of 81 production\nblocker sites and pretix's snippet split says roughly three quarters of those\nare genuine field writes (invoice.foreign_currency_rate_date = rate.source_date\n-- 7a verbatim). Capture-mediated closure, the ONE shape 7b forbids, is still\nZERO confirmed; one unverified Go candidate (prometheus factorySPr := func...)\nis held back rather than counted. The 7b ruling is re-priced DOWN a third time\nand still does not need to be asked.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT MEASURE: apollo-server (typescript) and zoxide (rust) ran ZERO\n3a walks -- not \"walks that closed\". The walk needs a source and a sink in the\nSAME function and neither repo has one against this claims file. Go contributes\n12 sites, 3 production-path: too few to quote as a share. Def/use extractors\nexist for exactly four languages, so java repos cannot produce a 3a escape site\nat all and are not candidates for any version of this measurement.\n\nSTATUS UNCHANGED: still violated. What changed is the diagnosis. This item is\nnot primarily a 7a/7b scope collision -- it is a receiver-typing gap (INV-linub)\nplus an emission gap (INV-mumov) plus a genuinely-correct escape population,\nsitting behind an arm with no live consumer."  # 10d9
- op: discuss  # c55b
  at: '2026-08-06T18:29:18Z'  # c55b
  by: agent  # c55b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c55b
  clock: 8  # c55b
  nonce: c55b  # c55b
  message: "CORRECTION to my S4 note above, same day. The BARRIER-ARM MECHANISM I\ngave is wrong, and with it the claim that this item's blockers sit behind\nan arm made dead by a thin sanitizer catalogue.\n\nWHAT I CLAIMED, HOURS EARLIER TODAY: zero barrier-arm walks BECAUSE the\nshipped sanitizer vocabulary is crypto-only -- and therefore \"a sanitizer\ncatalogue ARMS the removal hazard; catalogue growth creates the danger, so\ngates land before catalogue growth.\"\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT ESTABLISHED: I never positive-controlled the zero. A null\nbelieved without demonstrating the instrument can produce a known positive is\nthe exact rule this repo has written down, and I violated it the same day I\nedited it into LIVE.md. It is also the shape of this area's own invalidated\n0.00% confident-negative figure: a tautology of the configuration.\n\nTWO PURPOSE-BUILT FIXTURES, BOTH ZERO WALKS:\n  v1  f = Fernet(key); plain = f.decrypt(tok); cipher = f.encrypt(plain);\n      pathlib.Path(...).write_text(cipher)\n      -> the crypto calls emit python:external:0-0:decrypt:external_symbol.\n         The receiver type is never inferred, so the SOURCE cannot match\n         cryptography.fernet.Fernet.decrypt and the SANITIZER cannot match\n         Fernet.encrypt. No flow exists for a barrier to guard.\n  v2  the same shape written MODULE-QUALIFIED, removing receiver typing as a\n      variable -> source and sanitizer now resolve\n      (python:cryptography.fernet.Fernet:0-0:decrypt:external_symbol), but the\n      SINK emits NO call edge at all: pathlib.Path(...).write_text(x) is an\n      attribute chain rooted at a CONSTRUCTOR RESULT. Still zero walks.\n\nSO THE BARRIER ARM IS UNREACHABLE FOR THE SAME ROOT CAUSE AS THE REST OF THIS\nAREA. Every sanitizer in the shipped catalogue is an INSTANCE METHOD --\nFernet.encrypt, AESGCM.encrypt, AEAD.Seal, Cipher.doFinal -- and\ninstance-method receiver typing is precisely what is broken (INV-linub). That\nis a THIRD symptom family from one root cause, alongside io-boundary catalogue\nreach and the section 4 summary lookup inside the walk.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES: the observation (0 barrier walks across 4 repos and 2 fixtures)\nand the practical verdict (an escape reclassification cannot move a finding\ntoday). WHAT DOES NOT: the mechanism, and every sequencing conclusion built on\nit. Catalogue growth CANNOT arm the hazard while receiver typing is broken.\nRECEIVER TYPING IS WHAT ARMS IT.\n\nSTILL OPEN, stated rather than papered over: I could not construct a barrier\nwalk in ANY configuration, so I cannot distinguish \"reachable, and nothing in\nthe corpus hits it\" from \"effectively dead on the DDG path\". There is also NO\nend-to-end test driving the barrier arm through propagate_taint_ddg -- the one\n'sanitized' test uses propagate_taint_structural. A positive control is a\nprerequisite for any future claim about this arm, mine included.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM. The re-scoped blocker population, the\nblocked-vs-all-walks correction, the per-walk reach of the\nexpression-read family (3 of 49), and the 7a/7b re-pricing all stand --\nnone of them depend on the barrier arm. What changes is the DIAGNOSIS of\nwhy nothing moves: it is not 'no sanitizers catalogued', it is INV-linub\nall the way down. This item is downstream of INV-linub on THREE distinct\npaths now, and re-measuring it before receiver typing is fixed means\nre-measuring a confounded population."  # c55b
- op: discuss  # bd01
  at: '2026-08-06T22:05:00Z'  # bd01
  by: agent  # bd01
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bd01
  clock: 9  # bd01
  nonce: bd01  # bd01
  message: "BARRIER-ARM MECHANISM CORRECTED A SECOND TIME, 2026-08-06 (dev 8b1a20cc1b,\nfiled as INV-funit). This thread carries a correction from earlier the same\nday saying \"receiver typing arms the suppression hazard, not catalogue\ngrowth\". THAT IS ALSO WRONG and is retracted; it was the second uncontrolled\naccount of the same zero.\n\nESTABLISHED WITH A POSITIVE CONTROL, which neither prior account had: the\nbarrier arm was blocked by _register_sanitizer_callers reading the NAME slot\nof the dst for receiver evidence that production analyzers put in the MODULE\nslot. The comparison was \"cryptography.fernet.Fernet.encrypt\" == \"encrypt\" --\nfalse by construction for every method-shaped sanitizer, i.e. the entire\nshipped catalogue -- so the INV-finoh call_construct==\"method\" refusal was\nunconditional in production.\n\nTwo probes differing ONLY in receiver form emit a BYTE-IDENTICAL dst\n(python:cryptography.fernet.Fernet:0-0:encrypt:external_symbol) and differ\nonly in meta.call_construct; that alone flipped sanitizer_lines from\n{(handler,'plaintext'): [7,7,7,7]} to {}. The idiomatic form reported\n\"violated\" at \"precise\" confidence about code that visibly encrypts, and now\nreports \"confirmed\" with the arm firing.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES FROM BOTH RETRACTED ACCOUNTS: WI-joluk's coverage gate landing\nbefore removal authority is still right. The section 3a arm still tests\n`is True`, so escape-reclassification and section 7a field sensitivity are\nstill structurally INERT for finding counts. INV-karud clause (b)'s zero is\nstill not independent corroboration.\n\nWHAT IS NEW AND DECISION-RELEVANT: the corpus holds ZERO plaintext sanitizer\ncall sites (pretix's single gate-reaching edge is a bare `encrypt`, correctly\nrefused; everywhere else is absence), and the module slot reaches taint only\nfor python and java. The sanitizer/barrier machinery has no live surface on\ntoday's corpus -- everything here is prevention whose value scales with a\ncatalogue that is not growing. That belongs in the exit-condition package."  # bd01
- op: discuss  # 1308
  at: '2026-08-08T21:03:19Z'  # 1308
  by: agent  # 1308
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1308
  clock: 10  # 1308
  nonce: '1308'  # 1308
  message: "STATEMENT CONFIRMED. Only the root_cause EXEMPLARS are wrong. Correcting a\nprior grading error on this item.\n\nA measurement pass graded this premise \"false\"; two adversarial re-measurements\noverturned that. It graded the root_cause exemplars, not the statement. The\nstatement (\"section 3a cannot refute because a large share of escape sites are\nnot calls\") is CONFIRMED on measured substrate: non-call share of blocked-walk\nsites = 48/72 unique pretix sites (66.7%), 48/53 (90.6%) excluding\nsource_undefined, 75/127 pooled over 4 repos.\n\nEXEMPLARS THAT ARE WRONG for Python substrate:\n  container subscript = 0\n  capture-mediated closure = 3, ALL in caddy (Go func-literals), 0 in Python\nThe largest all-paths family is assert_statement expression reads (37/72, test\npaths); the largest production family on pretix is assignment (6/15) then call\n(5/15). Genuine no_heir field writes = 6/73 all-paths (8.2%), 6/16 production.\n\nPOPULATION CORRECTION: the true population is 137 blocked / 68 production sites\nover 5 repos (pretix, mitmproxy, caddy, kserve, meson). A prior run reported\n73/16 -- 23.5% of it -- having dropped caddy (which IS on this box, under\ncurriculum_repos, 9s warm) and lost kserve+meson to ENOSPC.\n\n§7b IS NOT THE BINDING CONSTRAINT and does not need to be asked. Refutation is\nblocked by the §3a arm's `is True` test, not by escape shape: §3a returns False\n0/68 walks, and the barrier arm ran 0 times on every measured repo. So NO escape\nreclassification can move a finding today.\n\nRECEIVER TYPING DID NOT SHRINK THE ESCAPE POPULATION -- but that test is\nUNDER-POWERED and must not be quoted as a null: pretix is byte-identical across\narms (the treatment does not apply there at all; total_io_edges 1051 vs 1051),\nleaving mitmproxy as the only repo with both treatment and walks, at 1 site."  # 1308
